The parts of the round trip
stop_reason
Every response carries a stop reason : a short string saying why the
model stopped producing text. In Chapter 1 you never looked at it
because there was only one answer. Now there are several, and the
program branches on it.

Knowing before you send
You can price a request before making it.
const count = await client.messages.countTokens({
model: MODEL,
system: CONTEXT,
messages,
});
console.log(`this request would cost ${count.input_tokens} input tokens`);
countTokens runs the same tokenizer the real request would, costs noth-
ing, and returns the input side only. It is the right tool for two jobs:
deciding whether a document is small enough to send whole, and check-
ing a large prefix before you spend money caching it.

